---
import Chapter from './Chapter.astro';

const pipeline = [
  ['Record', 'audio file on the phone'],
  ['Transcribe', 'whisper-large-v3-turbo'],
  ['Summarise', 'gpt-oss-120b'],
  ['Sync', 'text only, to your account'],
];
---

<Chapter n="01" kicker="Imagine" title="You’re in the meeting, but you’re mostly taking notes.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr;gap:5cqw">
      <div class="card note">
        <span class="scrawl">decided: ship v2 on…</span>
        <span class="scrawl x">ask Sam re budget??</span>
        <span class="scrawl">action items:</span>
        <span class="scrawl x">– ……</span>
        <span class="scrawl">wait what did she say</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="label-sm">meeting app</span>
        <strong>Subscription</strong>
        <span class="mono sub">renews monthly</span>
        <span class="mono sub">your audio: on their servers</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="pcap">illustration</p>
  </Fragment>
  <p class="lede">
    Imagine you’re in a meeting that matters. You’re half listening, half writing down what was decided, and by the end
    your notes have more question marks than answers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix on offer is a subscription app that records everything and keeps it on a server you will never see. That’s
    where Scribe started: I kept losing the thread in meetings, and I didn’t want to rent my own conversations back.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="02" kicker="The problem" title="An AI app needs a key, and a key inside an app is already leaked.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg">
      <div class="term">
<span class="m">$ # anyone, with a copy of the APK</span>
$ unzip -p scribe.apk | strings | grep gsk_
<span class="r">gsk_••••••••••••••••••••••••••••</span>

<span class="m"># now it is their key, on your bill</span></div>
      <div class="tag">so the key never ships in the app</div>
    </div>
    <p class="pcap">illustration</p>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    Transcription and summaries run on Groq, and Groq needs an API key. The easy path is to put a shared key in the app.
    But an Android app can be unpacked by anyone who has a copy, and a key inside it can be pulled out in seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the product question had a security answer: how do people without their own key use Scribe, without the shared
    key ever touching their phone?
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="03" kicker="The solution" title="Press record. Get the decisions, not the transcript.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg" style="grid-template-columns:30cqw 6cqw 1fr;gap:3cqw">
      <div class="phone rec-phone">
        <span class="mono sub">Weekly sync · 00:31:07</span>
        <div class="wave">{Array.from({ length: 14 }, (_, i) => <i style={`height:${20 + ((i * 37) % 70)}%`} />)}</div>
        <div class="rec"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="label-sm">summary</span>
        <strong>Decisions</strong>
        <span class="sub">Ship v2 on the 14th</span>
        <strong>Action items</strong>
        <span class="sub">Sam: budget by Friday</span>
        <span class="chip mono">language detected · translate</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="pcap">illustration</p>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    Scribe records the meeting, transcribes it with Whisper, and writes a summary of decisions and action items. It
    detects the language and can translate both the transcript and the summary. Meetings go into folders, can be
    scheduled with reminders, and export to PDF.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recordings are stored only on the phone. Users can paste their own Groq key and pay per use. Everyone else goes
    through a server-side proxy that holds the shared key and only answers signed-in users.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="04" kicker="My role" title="I built and shipped it on my own.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="ledger">
      <div><span>3</span>public releases, with release notes</div>
      <div><span>2</span>edge functions guarding the shared key</div>
      <div><span>16k</span>lines of Dart, roughly</div>
      <div><span>1</span>developer</div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    I designed, built and released Scribe myself: the Flutter app, the Supabase schema and authentication, the two edge
    functions, and three releases on GitHub with notes for each.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="05" kicker="Under the hood" title="Audio stays home. Text syncs. The key stays on the server.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr;align-items:stretch">
      <div class="stack">
        {
          pipeline.map(([t, d]) => (
            <div class="card stage">
              <strong>{t}</strong>
              <span class="mono sub">{d}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="label-sm">stack</span>
        {['Flutter', 'provider', 'Supabase auth', 'Edge Functions', 'Groq', 'local notifications'].map((t) => <span class="sub">{t}</span>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two paths for every AI request:</strong> straight to Groq with the user’s own key, or through an edge function that checks the user is signed in, then adds the shared key on the server.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage by sensitivity:</strong> audio files are stored only on the device (they are sent to Groq to be transcribed, never kept in the cloud); transcripts, summaries, folders and schedules sync to the user’s account.</li>
    <li><strong>State with <code>provider</code>,</strong> using proxy providers that rebind when the signed-in user changes, so one account never sees another’s data.</li>
  </ul>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="06" kicker="The hard part" title="The calendar that came back empty.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg" style="justify-items:center;gap:2.4cqw">
      <div class="card wide warn">
        <span class="label-sm">real phones</span>
        <strong>calendar plugin returned empty data</strong>
        <span class="sub">Google Calendar, Samsung Calendar, or none at all</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="card wide">
        <strong>Scheduling moved inside the app</strong>
        <span class="sub">same behaviour on every phone</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    Scheduling first relied on the phone’s calendar through a Flutter plugin. On real hardware it returned empty data,
    and real phones ship Google Calendar, Samsung Calendar or no calendar at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rather than chase every vendor’s calendar, I moved scheduling into Scribe itself, with its own reminders five
    minutes before and at the start, and a “join now” that turns a scheduled meeting into a recording with one tap. It
    now behaves the same on every phone.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .card.note { gap: 2.6cqw; transform: rotate(-2deg); }
  .scrawl { font: italic 500 calc(3 * var(--t))/1.2 var(--sans); }
  .scrawl.x { text-decoration: line-through; color: var(--muted); }
  .rec-phone { align-items: center; gap: 4cqw; }
  .wave { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.6cqw; height: 16cqw; width: 100%; }
  .wave i { flex: 1; background: var(--ink); }
  .rec { margin-top: auto; width: 10cqw; height: 10cqw; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent); box-shadow: 0 0 0 1.4cqw rgb(255 90 31 / 0.2); }
  .chip { align-self: flex-start; margin-top: 1cqw; border: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 0.6cqw 1.2cqw; font-size: calc(2 * var(--t)); }
  .stack { display: grid; grid-auto-rows: 1fr; gap: 2cqw; }
  .stage { justify-content: center; }
  .wide { width: 76cqw; align-items: center; text-align: center; }
  .warn { border-color: var(--accent); }
</style>
